# BYTES audit — is "12 bytes" really 12 bytes?

## 1. VERDICT

No headline byte figure survives: "12/24/48 B/doc" counts packed sign bits only, while the reported qscale accuracies additionally require per-archive sigma + mu + a ~122 MB (f32 lower bound) per-archive query encoder that is never charged. Under symmetric deployed accounting the codes are ~188x LARGER than the BM25 index they claim a 5.83x win over.

The "858,624 bits / 0 difference" seal proves only doc-side sign equality at k=96 — not size, not the query path, not sigma, not k=192/384.

Two narrow corrections are exact and machine-checked: true doc-side cost is 12.86/25.72/51.43 B/doc with sigma (13.72/27.43/54.87 with the uncounted mu); the fair BM25 rival is also 22.5% CHEAPER than the quoted index.

Full report: `/mnt/c/Users/MDP/dev/llmzip-work/audit_hard_r2/bytes/BYTES_AUDIT.md`. Probes + JSON in the same dir. Source trees untouched (read-only imports only). Findings below use headings instead of a wide table so they stay readable; same content as the file.

## 2. Findings

### F1 — CRITICAL: headline bytes count bits only
Claim: "12/24/48 B/doc" (`LADDER.json` bytes_per_doc; REPORT/LADDER/DECISION tables; `ladder.py` k/8).
Check: read scorers (`ladder.py:144-154`; `arm_decision.py:27-32`); P4 JSON arithmetic; 8944 docs verified from source.
Result: headline counts packed bits only. Even in the report's own narrow boundary (packed+sigma f64) truth is 12.86/25.72/51.43 B/doc (+7.15%). Published integers are wrong.

### F2 — CRITICAL: encoder reverses the storage comparison
Claim: "codes+sigma 115,008 B vs BM25 index 670,511 B (5.83x)" (REPORT sec 4; FINAL_STATE costs).
Check: rebuilt RT05 pipeline k=96/192/384 (P1); all-10-archive dims (P2); BM25 both tokenizers (P3).
Result: encoder matrices alone total ~122 MB f32 = 1137x payload. Full lower bound ~126 MB (~14.1 KB/doc, range 7.2-34.4 KB/doc) vs BM25 index 0.67 MB. Symmetric ratio: ~188x AGAINST codes (~243x vs fair frozen BM25). The 5.83x win is an asymmetric-boundary artifact. PQ worker flagged this (`pq/REPORT.md:58-61`); never followed up.

### F3 — HIGH: mu counted nowhere
Claim: sigma is the only side state (`cost_audit.py:7,76-78`; "codes + sigma 115,008 B").
Check: `build()` centers docs AND queries by mu (`ladder.py:111-132`; frozen `:639-657`).
Result: mu (k floats/archive = 7,680 B f64 at k=96, same as sigma) is load-bearing for ALL arms yet in no total. Adds +0.86/1.72/3.43 B/doc.

### F4 — HIGH: byte label is scorer-blind
Claim: one label per k covers sym/qscale/asym/MED/ITQ/RAND.
Check: all scorers vs `LADDER.json` labels + `quant_math.py:517` assert.
Result: same "12 B" needs mu+encoder (sym), +sigma (qscale), +96 medians (MED), +96x96 R (ITQ/RAND: 36,864 f32 / 73,728 f64 per archive). The 3 pp qscale-over-sym gain and T3 asym-over-qscale inversion (61.09 vs 54.11 at "48 B") are bought with uncharged state.

### F5 — HIGH: mixed precisions flatter codes
Claim: sigma f64, R quoted f32-silently, float arms f32.
Check: `cost_audit.py:77` vs REPORT:136-137 (3.43x/7.50x = f32, unstated; worker gives both) vs `ladder.py:243` vs float64 caches.
Result: main text prints the smaller rotation multiple (f64 doubles it: 6.87x/15x). No test shows f32 preserves the 0.0000pp anchors, esp. the 1e-12-floored tiny-sigma regime. UNVERIFIED.

### F6 — MED: rival cheaper than quoted; LME scope inconsistent
Claim: BM25 cost 670,511 B compact / 1,450,229 B pickle.
Check: re-ran encoding exactly (P3, all 10 archives); added frozen-tokenizer variant.
Result: reproduced coarse numbers to the byte; fair (frozen, 65.67) index is SMALLER: 519,740 B compact (-22.5%). True narrow ratio vs compared rival: 4.52x, not 5.83x. LME cites Python-object IDF (351 MB) after rejecting pickle inflation on RealTalk.

### F7 — MED: gate seals less than it appears
Claim: "0 differing bits of 858,624" (k=96); "0 of 276,480" (T3).
Check: gate blocks (`ladder.py:194-200`; `t3:125-128`); P4: 858,624 = 8944x96.
Result: seals ONLY doc-sign equality at k=96 (T3: n<384 subset only). Q96 built-but-uncompared; no sigma/mu/scores/k=192/384. No size proof.

### F8 — LOW: hunted side channels came back clean
Checked: per-doc scalars (none — all per-archive), doc-float residuals in bit scores (none — doc side +-1 only), ID/length/order leaks (none — hash(archive,row) tie-break, `audit_baseline_lib.py:79-83`; lens unused on code path). PQ's 98,304 B shared codebook (~22.99 B/doc) is properly charged — the package's one honest per-arm total.

Sampling: P3/P4 EXHAUSTIVE (10 archives, both tokenizers, JSON math). P1 SAMPLE (RT05 x 3 k). P2 all-archive D dims, analytic SVD-k sizes. Round-1 left `cost_audit` explicitly unreviewed — this is new ground.

